✅ Inheritance vs. Composition — Why Composition is Preferred

🔷 1. Inheritance (IS-A relationship)
A child class inherits properties and behaviors from a parent class.

```java
class Dog extends Animal {} // Dog IS-A Animal
```

🔷 2. Composition (HAS-A relationship)
A class contains an instance of another class and uses it to achieve functionality.

```java
class Engine {}

class Car {
    private final Engine engine = new Engine(); // Car HAS-A Engine
}
```

────────────────────────────────────────
🔍 Why composition is often better than inheritance

| Reason                              | Inheritance                                | Composition                            |
| ----------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------ | -------------------------------------- |
| 🔒 **Tight coupling**               | Yes — child is tightly bound to parent     | No — more flexible and loosely coupled |
| 🔁 **Flexibility**                  | Difficult to change parent-child structure | You can easily swap out components     |
| 🧪 **Testing**                      | Harder to test in isolation                | Easier to mock and test                |
| 🔄 **Runtime Behavior**             | Fixed at compile time                      | Can change behavior at runtime         |
| 🧬 **Reusability**                  | Inherits everything (even if not needed)   | Uses only what it needs                |
| ⚠️ **Fragile Base Class Problem**   | Changes in parent can break child          | Less risk, as components are separate  |
| 🔄 **Multiple Inheritance Support** | Not allowed in Java (classes)              | Easily done with multiple fields       |

────────────────────────────────────────
🔧 Real-life Example

🔹 Inheritance (forced coupling):

```java
class Printer {
    void print() { /* ... */ }
}

class SmartPrinter extends Printer {
    void scan() { /* ... */ }
}
```

Problem: `SmartPrinter` is forced to inherit everything from `Printer`, even things it doesn't need.

🔹 Composition (flexible):

```java
class Printer {
    void print() { /* ... */ }
}

class Scanner {
    void scan() { /* ... */ }
}

class SmartPrinter {
    private final Printer printer = new Printer();
    private final Scanner scanner = new Scanner();

    void print() { printer.print(); } // delegation
    void scan() { scanner.scan(); }
}
```

✅ Now `SmartPrinter` has only what it needs, and you can replace components easily.

────────────────────────────────────────
🧭 When to still use Inheritance
Use inheritance when there is a true **IS-A** relationship and you need polymorphism:
- `ArrayList` IS-A `List` / `AbstractList` hierarchy
- `Dog` IS-A `Animal` with shared behavior + overriding

Use composition when you mainly want **reuse** or optional capabilities (HAS-A):
- `Car` HAS-A `Engine`
- `PaymentService` HAS-A `PaymentProcessor`

Rule of thumb: **“Favor composition over inheritance”** (GoF / SOLID-friendly), but don’t force it when IS-A + polymorphism is the real model.

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ Summary (Interview Answer)
"While inheritance promotes code reuse, it can lead to tight coupling and fragile designs.
Composition offers greater flexibility, better encapsulation, and easier testing by allowing classes to use only what they need.
That’s why modern design principles favor composition over inheritance — use inheritance mainly for true IS-A relationships and polymorphism."

────────────────────────────────────────
🌱 Spring example: Interface + Composition + Delegation (together)

Typical flow: `Controller` → `Service` interface → `ServiceImpl`

```java
public interface UserService {
    User getUser(Long id);
}

@Service
public class UserServiceImpl implements UserService {
    public User getUser(Long id) {
        // business logic
        return new User(id, "Afham");
    }
}

@RestController
public class UserController {
    private final UserService userService; // Composition (HAS-A) + depend on interface

    // Constructor injection
    public UserController(UserService userService) {
        this.userService = userService;
    }

    @GetMapping("/users/{id}")
    public User getUser(@PathVariable Long id) {
        return userService.getUser(id); // Delegation (hand off work)
    }
}
```

What you are using here:
| Piece                         | Concept                                      |
| ----------------------------- | -------------------------------------------- |
| `UserService` interface       | Abstraction / contract                       |
| `UserServiceImpl implements`  | Polymorphism (swap impl easily / mock in test) |
| Controller field + ctor inject| **Composition** (Controller HAS-A UserService) |
| `userService.getUser(...)`    | **Delegation** (controller forwards the work) |

➡️ Yes: in Spring apps you commonly use **both composition and delegation**, with an **interface** for loose coupling.
➡️ This is **not inheritance** between Controller and Service (`Controller` does not `extend` Service).

🎤 Quick interview traps
1) Composition ≠ always better; wrong IS-A modeling is the real problem.
2) Composition often works with **delegation** (forward calls to the contained object).
3) Inheritance is compile-time coupling; composition can swap implementations at runtime (especially via interfaces).
4) Prefer composition for reuse; prefer inheritance for polymorphic type hierarchies.
5) Constructor injection of a service interface = composition + delegation, not inheritance.
